<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Kamil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1218">1218</a>–<a href="/years/1238">1238</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Kamil was an Ayyubid sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> who reigned from 1218 to 1238, a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>. He promoted trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-city-states">Italian city-states</a>. In 1229 he surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-jaffa-1229">Treaty of Jaffa</a>, an act that damaged Ayyubid prestige.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-kamil.jpg" alt="al-Kamil" width="199" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benozzo Gozzoli (1420–1497) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benozzo_Gozzoli_-_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_St_Francis,_Al-Kamil_detail_(Scene_10,_north_wall)_-_WGA10241.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saladin's nephew, Sultan <b>al-Kamil</b>, later handed Jerusalem to Emperor Frederick II by treaty in 1229, in return for help against his own Ayyubid relatives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Frederick II regained Jerusalem by treaty with the Ayyubid sultan <b>al-Kamil</b> in 1229, but the city was sacked by Khwarazmian troops in 1244 and never recovered.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Al-Kamil</b>, a nephew of Saladin, became the fourth Ayyubid sultan of Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-31">31 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Sixth Crusade, Emperor Frederick II agreed a ten-year truce with the Ayyubid Sultan <b>al-Kamil</b> that returned Jerusalem to Christian control without a battle.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-18">18 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Caught between the Ayyubids and the Sultanate of Rum, the dynasty lost Hasankeyf and Amid to the Ayyubid <b>al-Kamil</b> in 1232, and the Ayyubids of Hisn Kayfa took over.</blockquote><a href="/state/artuqids-of-hasankeyf">Artuqids of Hasankeyf</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1232 the Ayyubid sultan <b>al-Kamil</b> took Hisn Kayfa and Amid from the Artuqids of Hasankeyf and gave them to his son al-Salih Ayyub.</blockquote><a href="/state/ayyubids-of-hisn-kayfa">Ayyubids of Hisn Kayfa</a></li></ul></section>
